.TH CLOUDINIT 1
.SH NAME
cloudinit \- configure a cloud instance's network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cloudinit
[
.B -v
]
[
.BI -m " metadataip"
]
[
.BI -n " nifc"
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cloudinit
binds the IP stack into
.BR /net ,
attaches the first Ethernet interface that answers, takes a DHCP lease,
and adds a host route to the metadata service.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.B -v
Report each step.
.TP
.BI -m " metadataip"
The metadata service's address, routed explicitly.  The default is
.BR 169.254.169.254 .
.TP
.BI -n " nifc"
How many Ethernet interfaces to try.  The default is 3.
.PP
None of this can be done from a shell.
.B #I
has to be bound into
.B /net
before there is an IP stack; the interface that
.B /net/ipifc/clone
allocates lasts only while that file descriptor stays open, so the
process that configures it must hold it; and the metadata service is on
a link-local address that no lease covers, so it needs a route of its
own through the lease's gateway.
.PP
The lease is not released.  The
.B pc64dev
configuration's startup does release it, because it is testing the
exchange rather than keeping the address; an instance that means to
keep its address must not.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/cloudinit.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cloudfs (4),
.IR hget (1),
.IR dhcp (8)
.SH BUGS
It does not work on Compute Engine, and the reason is worth stating.
Google's DHCP hands out a /32 netmask with a gateway outside it, and
expects the guest to install a link-scope route to that gateway before
a default route through it can resolve.  This kernel's IP stack takes
the lease and then cannot reach the gateway: a ping to it is lost, so
nothing off the instance is reachable, the metadata service included.
Under QEMU, whose DHCP hands out a conventional netmask, the same code
takes a lease, pings its gateway and fetches over TCP without trouble.
Fixing it means teaching the stack, or this program, how to make an
off-link gateway reachable.
